1. Exited MCP leaders incur no TERM or grace

OwnedChild::exited observes waitid(WEXITED|WNOWAIT|WNOHANG), retaining identity.
An earlier fenced wait also needs no further grace. finish_child skips TERM
and grace for an exited leader. A running leader receives TERM, then exit is
polled in requested sleeps of at most 5 ms, up to the 1 s deadline. Exit or an
observation error proceeds directly to the same fenced group KILL, retirement
and wait. Lost notifications have the same cleanup obligation.

mcp_finish_exited_leader_skips_term_and_grace_but_kills_members uses a real
stubborn-group server. Only its leader is killed first and observed with
WNOWAIT; a member remains present before finish_child. Cleanup must complete
in one future poll (test-only cooperative budgeting disabled), with zero TERM
calls and zero grace entries. It must kill the member, leave no late signal,
and preserve the disposable foreign group. The single-poll assertion catches
even an uninstrumented tokio::sleep. No stopwatch or percentile is used.
The instrumented old unconditional TERM/one-second flow fails with exit 101
at “already-exited MCP cleanup sent TERM” (actual 1, expected 0). Restoring the
fix passes. Separate TERM and direct-sleep controls each fail their named
assertion, then pass when restored.

2. Already-fenced idempotence is labelled accurately

The former missing-id witness is renamed
mcp_finish_already_fenced_child_is_idempotent_and_keeps_foreign_group_safe.
It forces actual child.id()==None after an owned wait, then proves idempotence,
zero TERM/sleep attempts and foreign-group safety. It does not attribute the
fixture's earlier member kill to finish_child. The new exited-but-unreaped
test proves finish_child's live-member obligation. The failed-handoff test
still forces a real receiver error and requires cleanup of live members.

3. Registration drives an actual signal

registered_at_reap has been removed. signal_registered_group is the actual
production handler CAS/dispatch primitive. The fixture invokes that same
action after the kernel wait releases the leader, using its captured pid so
a slot reused for another pid is untouched. A stale registration invokes the
signal callback and signals the fixture's own foreign group. Assertions check
the signal and foreign process, rather than sampling a flag after retirement.

HarnessProcessLifecycle::SignalEmptyGroup reads registered. The stale-reap
variant must reach leader=reaped, registered=1, signal_to_empty=1 and violate
NoSignalToEmptyGroup. The assignment-only NoRegistrationAfterReap invariant
was removed from this model; correct-state retirement is still checked by the
unit/nonvacuity witnesses. Java must now reach an actual signal to fail.
HarnessMcpGroupClose retains cleanup checks for delivered/lost notifications.
verification/contracts.toml and coverage.toml record the structural timing
witness and abstraction boundaries.

